public int search(int[] nums, int target) {
    int left = 0;
    int right = nums.length - 1;
    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = (left + right) / 2;
        if (nums[mid] == target) {
            return mid;
        }
        if (nums[mid] >= nums[left]) { // left part is sorted
            if (target >= nums[left] && target < nums[mid]) {
                right = mid - 1;
            } else {
                left = mid + 1;
            }
        } else { // right part is sorted
            if (target > nums[mid] && target <= nums[right]) {
                left = mid + 1;
            } else {
                right = mid - 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return -1;
}
```
The algorithm implemented is known as the "binary search algorithm with rotation" and is used to search for an element in a rotated sorted matrix. It is efficient in terms of time complexity and works by exploiting the fact that the rotated array is still sorted, the only difference is that the left part is sorted in ascending order, while the right part is sorted in descending order.

The algorithm starts by finding the midpoint of the array and checking if it's equal to the target. If it is, it returns the index of the midpoint. If not, it checks if the midpoint is in the left part of the array or the right part and adjusts the left and right pointers accordingly.

It is important to notice that the time complexity of the algorithm is O(log n) and it is mentioned in the problem statement.

